            <ArticleTitle>Symptomatic status and pattern of employment during a five-year period following myocardial revascularization for angina.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>383-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The employment status of 100 consecutive male patients between the ages of 30 and 60 years (mean, 49.5 years) operated on for angina and followed for a minimum of 5 years was analysed. There were no early and 4 late deaths. The probability of employment at 1 year was 90%, which was similar to that of a sample from the general population matched for age. This rate, however, showed steady, slow deterioration to 87.6% at 2 years, 82.1% at 3 years, 75.7% at 4 years, and 73.4+ at 5 years. Seventeen patients retired over the 5-year period. There was no correlation between the rate of employment and age (under or over 50) at operation, previous myocardial infarction, or number of grafts inserted.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
